1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,949
This video is about lexical analysis, the first phase of a compiler.

2
00:00:05,399 --> 00:00:19,821
You learn what tokens and lexemes are, how to tokenize a program by hand, and the maximal munch rule. Then we look at real languages where lexing is hard: FORTRAN, PL/I and C plus plus.

3
00:00:20,271 --> 00:00:26,479
Here is the compiler pipeline from video 1. Five phases, and the data between them.

4
00:00:26,929 --> 00:00:31,451
This video is about phase 1, the lexical analyzer, or lexer.

5
00:00:31,901 --> 00:00:38,621
The lexer reads the input program and writes a stream of tokens. The parser then reads the tokens.

6
00:00:39,071 --> 00:00:50,229
What does a lexer do? Here is a small program. You see four lines with indentation: if i equals equals j, z equals 0, else z equals 1.

7
00:00:50,679 --> 00:01:01,857
But the lexer does not see lines. The input is one string of characters. Backslash t is a tab. Backslash n is a newline. These are shown in orange.

8
00:01:02,307 --> 00:01:09,817
The goal is to partition this string into substrings. Each substring is a lexeme, and its class is a token.

9
00:01:10,267 --> 00:01:15,856
The lines and the indentation exist only as tab and newline bytes in the string.

10
00:01:16,306 --> 00:01:30,514
A program is a sequence of bytes in a file. Some bytes have no glyph: tab, newline and carriage return. So we write them with an escape sequence. Here is the first line of the example in its written form.

11
00:01:30,964 --> 00:01:41,993
Count the characters on the page: 15. The slide says 16 characters. Our count of the written form gives 15. The byte count is the number that matters.

12
00:01:42,443 --> 00:01:49,483
In the file, the same line is 13 bytes. The first byte is a tab. The last byte is a newline.

13
00:01:49,933 --> 00:02:06,659
An escape sequence is two characters on the page, but one byte in the file. Printed as blank space, a tab is ambiguous: is it one tab or four spaces? Windows ends each line with backslash r backslash n, which is two bytes.

14
00:02:07,109 --> 00:02:22,085
Now tokenize the input by hand, as on the slide. Read the input one byte at a time. Write each lexeme, and next to it, its token: whitespace, keyword, identifier, integer, relation or punctuation.

15
00:02:22,535 --> 00:02:24,177
Pause the video and try it.

16
00:02:24,627 --> 00:02:32,969
Here are the first 15 bytes, with their positions. Read from left to right, and take the longest lexeme that is a token.

17
00:02:33,419 --> 00:02:41,952
Byte 0 is a tab. The next byte, i, is not white space. So the lexeme is one tab, and its token is whitespace.

18
00:02:42,402 --> 00:02:53,581
Next, i. It could be an identifier, but it can still grow. i f is also a token. The next byte is a space, so the lexeme stops. i f is a keyword.

19
00:02:54,031 --> 00:03:03,204
A space: whitespace. An open parenthesis: punctuation. Then i, an identifier. It cannot grow, because a space follows.

20
00:03:03,654 --> 00:03:17,627
A space. Then equals. One equals sign is a token, but it can grow: equals equals is a relation. The next byte is a space. So the lexeme is equals equals, and the token is relation.

21
00:03:18,077 --> 00:03:22,003
A space, the identifier j, and the close parenthesis.

22
00:03:22,453 --> 00:03:32,906
Last, the newline and the two tabs. All three are white space, so they make one lexeme: whitespace. These are the 11 rows of the slide answer.

23
00:03:33,356 --> 00:03:36,257
Here is the whole input: 36 bytes.

24
00:03:36,707 --> 00:03:48,057
Continue the same way to the end. The bars show the lexemes, coloured by token. There are 26 lexemes: 11 are whitespace, and 15 are other tokens.

25
00:03:48,507 --> 00:03:58,384
The lexer usually drops the whitespace. So the parser gets 15 tokens: if, open parenthesis, i, equals equals, j, and so on.

26
00:03:58,834 --> 00:04:06,322
Each token carries its lexeme. For example, keyword with lexeme if, and identifier with lexeme i.

27
00:04:06,772 --> 00:04:15,305
What is a token? A token is a syntactic category. In English, the categories are noun, verb, adjective, and so on.

28
00:04:15,755 --> 00:04:22,731
In a programming language, the categories are identifier, integer, keyword, whitespace, and so on.

29
00:04:23,181 --> 00:04:31,267
So a token is a category, and a lexeme is one string in that category. i is a lexeme. Identifier is its token.

30
00:04:31,717 --> 00:04:42,533
Each token is a set of strings. Identifier: strings of letters or digits that start with a letter. Keyword: else, or if, or begin, and so on.

31
00:04:42,983 --> 00:04:59,004
Integer: a non-empty string of digits. Whitespace: a non-empty sequence of blanks, newlines and tabs. Relation: equals equals, less than, less than or equal. And each punctuation mark is a token of its own.

32
00:04:59,454 --> 00:05:14,515
There is a problem. The string if is in two sets. It is a keyword, and it also matches the identifier rule. The slide answer makes if a keyword. So a keyword wins over an identifier. The same is true for else.

33
00:05:14,965 --> 00:05:21,344
A quick check. For each string, which token set contains it? Try them before the answers appear.

34
00:05:21,794 --> 00:05:33,314
z 1 starts with a letter, so it is an identifier. 0 0 7 is a non-empty string of digits: an integer. A newline and a tab are white space.

35
00:05:33,764 --> 00:05:50,788
Less than or equal is a relation. But 1 z is in no set. It starts with a digit, so it is not an identifier, and it has a letter, so it is not an integer. A lexer cuts it into two lexemes: the integer 1, then the identifier z.

36
00:05:51,238 --> 00:06:10,246
What are tokens for? They classify substrings of the program by their role. The output of the lexer is a stream of tokens, and that stream is the input of the parser. The parser relies on the token distinctions. For example, it treats an identifier differently from a keyword.

37
00:06:10,696 --> 00:06:36,381
To design a lexer, step 1: define a finite set of tokens. The tokens describe all items of interest. The choice depends on the language and on the design of the parser. For our example: integer, keyword, relation, identifier, whitespace, and four single tokens. Here open parenthesis, close parenthesis, equals and semicolon are tokens, not characters.

38
00:06:36,831 --> 00:06:45,962
Step 2: describe which strings belong to each token. The exact way to do this is a regular expression. That is the next video.

39
00:06:46,412 --> 00:06:50,039
How is a lexer implemented? It must do two things.

40
00:06:50,489 --> 00:06:59,321
First, recognize the substrings that correspond to tokens. For example, it recognizes that i f is in the keyword set.

41
00:06:59,771 --> 00:07:12,165
Second, return the token and its value, the lexeme. The lexeme is the substring that was tokenized. For example, keyword with lexeme if, and identifier with lexeme i.

42
00:07:12,615 --> 00:07:21,554
The lexer usually discards uninteresting tokens, which do not contribute to parsing. Examples are white space and comments.

43
00:07:22,004 --> 00:07:30,665
Is lexing as easy as it sounds? Not quite. Look at some history. In FORTRAN, the rule is: whitespace is insignificant.

44
00:07:31,115 --> 00:07:40,203
So V A R 1 is the same as V A, three spaces, R 1. The lexer removes the blanks, and both give one identifier.

45
00:07:40,653 --> 00:07:53,133
This means a blank cannot end a token. The slides call it a terrible design. The rule came from punch cards: the operators who typed the cards were not accurate with spaces.

46
00:07:53,583 --> 00:08:09,199
This example is optional. It is not on the slides, but it shows why the FORTRAN rule is a problem. Take the line D O 5 I equals 1 comma 25. With the blanks removed, the lexer sees these characters.

47
00:08:09,649 --> 00:08:17,969
Now take D O 5 I equals 1 point 25. Only one character is different: a point instead of a comma.

48
00:08:18,419 --> 00:08:29,427
The first line is a loop: the keyword D O, the label 5, and the variable I. The second line is an assignment to one variable called D O 5 I.

49
00:08:29,877 --> 00:08:38,325
The first six characters are the same. To decide if D O is a keyword, the lexer must look ahead to the comma or the point.

50
00:08:38,775 --> 00:08:55,607
The slides make two points. First, the lexer reads left to right and recognizes one token at a time. Second, lookahead may be needed to decide where one token ends and the next token begins. Even our simple example has this issue.

51
00:08:56,057 --> 00:09:01,753
Start at i. i is an identifier. But it may be the start of if, so keep going.

52
00:09:02,203 --> 00:09:06,299
i f is a keyword, and a longer token may still follow.

53
00:09:06,749 --> 00:09:13,555
The next character is a space. i f space is not a token. So stop, and emit the keyword if.

54
00:09:14,005 --> 00:09:21,685
Later, the lexer reaches an equals sign. One equals is a token, but it may be the start of equals equals.

55
00:09:22,135 --> 00:09:24,609
Equals equals is a relation.

56
00:09:25,059 --> 00:09:30,499
Equals equals space is not a token. So emit the relation equals equals.

57
00:09:30,949 --> 00:09:42,192
This is the rule of maximal munch, or longest match. Keep extending the current token while it can still grow. Emit it when the next character cannot extend it.

58
00:09:42,642 --> 00:09:54,994
Now an exercise. Tokenize i f 1 less than or equal 2 0 semicolon. Use maximal munch and the tokens of the slides. Give each lexeme and its token.

59
00:09:55,444 --> 00:09:57,087
Pause the video and try it.

60
00:09:57,537 --> 00:10:01,590
Start at byte 0. Grow the lexeme while it is still a token.

61
00:10:02,040 --> 00:10:11,597
i is an identifier. i f is a keyword. i f 1 is an identifier, because it is letters and digits that start with a letter.

62
00:10:12,047 --> 00:10:17,978
i f 1 less than is not a token. So emit i f 1 as one identifier.

63
00:10:18,428 --> 00:10:28,348
Next, less than is a relation, and less than or equal is also a relation. Less than or equal 2 is not a token. Emit the relation.

64
00:10:28,798 --> 00:10:36,905
Next, 2, then 2 0: an integer. 2 0 semicolon is not a token. Emit the integer 2 0.

65
00:10:37,355 --> 00:10:52,480
Last, the semicolon is punctuation. So there are four tokens. A common error is to take the keyword if and then the integer 1. Maximal munch takes the longest match, so i f 1 is one identifier.

66
00:10:52,930 --> 00:11:07,287
In PL/I, keywords are not reserved. You may use a keyword as a variable name. Here is a legal statement: IF ELSE THEN THEN equals ELSE semicolon, ELSE ELSE equals THEN. Which words are keywords?

67
00:11:07,737 --> 00:11:09,977
Pause the video and find the keywords.

68
00:11:10,427 --> 00:11:23,078
The statement has the form: IF condition THEN statement ELSE statement. So the first IF is a keyword. The next word, ELSE, is the condition: a variable. Then THEN is a keyword.

69
00:11:23,528 --> 00:11:28,456
THEN equals ELSE is an assignment. Here THEN and ELSE are both variables.

70
00:11:28,906 --> 00:11:44,330
After the semicolon, ELSE is a keyword. Then ELSE equals THEN is another assignment of two variables. So only 3 of the 8 words are keywords. The same word has different tokens, and only the context decides.

71
00:11:44,780 --> 00:11:53,527
Another PL/I problem is the declaration: DECLARE, open parenthesis, ARG 1 to ARG N, close parenthesis.

72
00:11:53,977 --> 00:12:01,251
The lexer cannot tell whether DECLARE is a keyword or an array reference until after the close parenthesis.

73
00:12:01,701 --> 00:12:13,285
If it is a declaration, DECLARE is a keyword. If it is an array reference, DECLARE is an identifier. N can be any number, so the lexer needs arbitrary lookahead.

74
00:12:13,735 --> 00:12:26,194
The problems continue today, in C plus plus. A template uses angle brackets: Foo of Bar. And greater than greater than is the right shift operator: 8 shifted right by 1.

75
00:12:26,644 --> 00:12:38,121
Now nest two templates: Foo of Bar of Bazz, with two closing brackets. Maximal munch takes the longest match. So the lexer makes one token, the shift operator.

76
00:12:38,571 --> 00:12:46,230
But the parser needs two closing brackets, one for each template. This is a conflict between the lexer and the grammar.

77
00:12:46,680 --> 00:13:03,469
Before C plus plus 11, the workaround was an extra space between the two brackets. The space stops the munch, so the lexer makes two tokens. We checked this: without the space, the old standard gives an error. With the space, the code compiles.

78
00:13:03,919 --> 00:13:10,341
C plus plus 11 fixed the language, not the lexer. The lexer still makes one shift token.

79
00:13:10,791 --> 00:13:21,671
The parser splits the shift token into two closing brackets when it is inside template arguments. The first closes Bar of Bazz. The second closes Foo.

80
00:13:22,121 --> 00:13:39,507
What if you want a real shift inside a template argument? Use parentheses. Value of, open parenthesis, 8 shift 1, close parenthesis. 8 shifted right by 1 is 4, so this is Value of 4. We compiled and ran this to check it.

81
00:13:39,957 --> 00:13:51,328
The rule is in the C plus plus 11 standard, section 14.2. Inside template arguments, the first non-nested shift token splits into two.

82
00:13:51,778 --> 00:13:58,498
To summarize. The input of the lexer is a string of bytes. A tab and a newline are one byte each.

83
00:13:58,948 --> 00:14:07,396
The goal of lexical analysis is to partition the input string into lexemes, and to identify the token of each lexeme.

84
00:14:07,846 --> 00:14:16,465
A token is a set of strings. A keyword wins over an identifier. The lexer usually discards white space and comments.

85
00:14:16,915 --> 00:14:26,259
The lexer scans left to right, one token at a time. With maximal munch, it takes the longest match, so lookahead is sometimes needed.

86
00:14:26,709 --> 00:14:41,727
Real languages make this hard: blanks in FORTRAN, keywords and DECLARE in PL/I, and the shift token in C plus plus. Next video: regular expressions, the exact way to describe each token.
